00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
 Hey everyone, Quincy Larson here with freeCodeCamp.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.393
I am thrilled to introduce the 2021 Data Science curriculum expansion.

00:00:08.393 --> 00:00:14.769
This is going to be massive. We've been working on it for several months.

00:00:14.769 --> 00:00:20.174
This is actually something I've wanted to do for years. We're going to teach new certifications

00:00:20.174 --> 00:00:25.557
in mathematics, data science, and machine learning.

00:00:25.557 --> 00:00:32.877
And in order to teach these complicated, hierarchical topics, we're going to have to pull out all the stops

00:00:32.877 --> 00:00:36.805
and roll out some really cool new learning experiences.

00:00:36.805 --> 00:00:43.136
First of all, I want to remind everybody that we are raising money to be able to bring on some additional instructional staff

00:00:43.136 --> 00:00:48.916
So that we can design these curricula.

00:00:48.916 --> 00:00:51.852
There are a lot of topics that we ourselves have expertise on. For example

00:00:51.852 --> 00:00:59.072
Beau has written an entire course for Manning that's focused on deep learning

00:00:59.072 --> 00:01:04.475
so he knows a lot about neural networks and stuff like that. But we don't have as much 

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:08.229
expertise in supervised learning or unsupervised learning. We don't have a lot of expertise

00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:13.248
in teaching calculus or linear algebra so we're looking for some professors who do have experience

00:01:13.248 --> 00:01:18.855
whom we can bring on to help design some of those projects.

00:01:18.855 --> 00:01:24.591
So if you decide that you're willing to donate to our nonprofit...

00:01:24.591 --> 00:01:30.209
If you're already donating $5 per month as one of the 7,000 people who support our nonprofit's mission

00:01:30.209 --> 00:01:36.152
fantastic. You don't have to donate but if you do want to donate we would absolutely welcome it.

00:01:36.152 --> 00:01:42.378
And the big news is Darrell Silver is matching all donations 100% up to $150,000.

00:01:42.378 --> 00:01:51.519
So if you give $100 it's going to be like you gave $200. Because Darrell is going to give $100 as well to match that.

00:01:51.519 --> 00:02:03.859
Of course freeCodeCamp is a donor-supported nonprofit, 501(c)(3), tax exempt.

00:02:03.859 --> 00:02:07.662
Which menas that there's a good chance that you'll be able to deduct this from your taxes

00:02:07.662 --> 00:02:11.929
depending on which country you're living in as well. And I can get you a donation receipt for that as well.

00:02:11.929 --> 00:02:17.630
So be sure to make a donation to our nonprofit for this very special purpose of us helping build this new

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:23.538
curriculum. Now, I'm going to talk about the curriculum. There's quite a bit to cover

00:02:23.538 --> 00:02:28.439
and there are going to be a few demos. So bear with me. First, what is the current curriculum? What does it look like?

00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:37.883
Well, the curriculum represents the shortest path to getting a developer job. And here's our landing page.

00:02:37.883 --> 00:02:42.880
Let's go to the curriculum section here.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.880
And just because we can, let's switch it to night mode so it'll be a little easier on the eyes.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:56.363
So, after this nice inspirational quote, we're going to see that the current curriculum is mostly web development.

00:02:56.363 --> 00:03:06.423
We do have some Python development in here, but we're going to dramatically expand this.

00:03:06.423 --> 00:03:08.423
So what is the new curriculum going to look like? Well, the first thing to note is that the curriculum

00:03:08.423 --> 00:03:18.430
is a line. It's linear. We're not trying to introduce branching paths. I personally strongly believe that data science

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:28.111
is a natural progression after you've worked as a developer. You go back, you learn some additional math

00:03:28.111 --> 00:03:32.566
you learn some additional skills and then you can become a data scientist. And if you really really

00:03:32.566 --> 00:03:41.692
want to get into it, you can become a machine learning engineer. And you're going to learn even more math for that.

00:03:41.692 --> 00:03:50.670
The logical hopping off points for being able to go out and get a web developer job – one of the full stack developer jobs out there

00:03:50.670 --> 00:04:01.755
About half of all developer jobs are web development jobs. And if you're a full stack developer you should be able to do

00:04:01.755 --> 00:04:10.445
pretty much anything. You may need to learn some new skills on the job, but this will give you a general framework.

00:04:10.445 --> 00:04:16.638
Responsive web design, JavaScript algorithms and data structures, front end libraries

00:04:16.638 --> 00:04:23.590
There's a new certification we're very proud of that we're going to demo in a few minutes where you're in VS Code and you're learning Bash and SQL and Postgres and Git

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:31.795
and lots of Linux commands interactively. Very cool. In VS Code. In a browser. On freeCodeCamp

00:04:31.795 --> 00:04:43.205
So Tom's going to demo that in a minute. Then APIs and Microservices, Quality Assurance, Information Security.

00:04:43.205 --> 00:04:48.039
All really important web development concepts.

00:04:48.039 --> 00:04:55.793
Then if you want to stick around, or if you already have a web development job and you want to expand your skills

00:04:55.793 --> 00:05:02.095
Data science. We use the Python ecosystem extensively. It's very powerful. You can do pretty much everything in Python that you need to do for data science

00:05:02.095 --> 00:05:14.338
And it's growing very fast. There's this huge critical mass of talent that's contributing to the Python ecosystem.

00:05:14.338 --> 00:05:22.517
So we're going to teach scientific computing with Python. Then we're going to get into teaching math.

00:05:22.517 --> 00:05:28.657
And the way we're teaching math is with Jupyter Notebooks. Eric is going to demo what that looks like in just a moment.

00:05:28.657 --> 00:05:35.701
But basically we've got tests that run within Jupyter Notebook, and you'll be able to basically learn math by coding.

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:44.504
You'll take a mathematical expresson and you'll implement that with code. Instead of the traditional way

00:05:44.504 --> 00:05:49.841
You might write things out with pencil and paper and move things around – there's a lot of value in that too

00:05:49.841 --> 00:05:57.503
But we're going to assume that people are busy adults and don't have time to go back to school and learn math

00:05:57.503 --> 00:06:05.067
The way you would have learned it in primary or secondary school. They want to jump in and learn it

00:06:05.067 --> 00:06:11.629
In the most practical and time-effective way, in a way that's integrated with the contemporary tools we have.

00:06:11.629 --> 00:06:17.186
Nowadays unless you're out on a desert island, you probably have access to some kind of computer that may be very powerful

00:06:17.186 --> 00:06:25.456
That you can use to do math. And so it's less about knowing how to do math by hand, and more about knowing how to use computers

00:06:25.456 --> 00:06:34.104
to get whatever you need done. And that involves you understanding concepts. And we're going to teach you those concepts.

00:06:34.104 --> 00:06:42.298
We're going to drill on those heavily through a series of tons of rigorous projects.

00:06:42.298 --> 00:06:46.845
But the way you're going to learn math is a bit faster than going back and learning 12 years of math and then some university level math as well.

00:06:46.845 --> 00:06:54.107
Instead, you're going to jump straight into it. But this will assume you haven't done math since high school.

00:06:54.107 --> 00:07:00.789
And that you may not have even done that much math in high school.

00:07:00.789 --> 00:07:07.013
That's how basic we're going to start here. We're going to do foundational math and then we're going to get into

00:07:07.013 --> 00:07:10.389
Algorithmic problem solving. We'll take the math you've learned and apply it toward learning Greedy Algorithms, Divide and Conquer Algorithms,

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:19.776
Dynamic Programming. Things like that. Then we get into the real work horse of data science, which is statistics.

00:07:19.776 --> 00:07:25.296
And here we're going to teach you in the descriptive statistics section all the statistics you can learn without knowing calculus.

00:07:25.296 --> 00:07:34.540
And then we're going to turn around and apply that data engineering and exploratory data analysis.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:42.805
With this, you're probably going to have the basic data science skills you need to be able to go to a small business or startup and start

00:07:42.805 --> 00:07:48.994
gleaning insights from their data as a data scientist.

00:07:48.994 --> 00:07:52.696
But if you want to work for a government or like a giant corporation that has tons of data. "Big data" as they used to call it. I don't know if they still call it that.

00:07:52.696 --> 00:08:03.806
Lots of data, essentially. American Express level data or Visa level data or The IRS or something like that.

00:08:03.806 --> 00:08:13.689
Those kinds of data sets. NASA or Cern Data? You're going to need a lot more than that. You're going to need to know calculus.

00:08:13.689 --> 00:08:27.252
That's the first thing we're going to teach you in the advanced data science section. Single Variable calculus.

00:08:27.252 --> 00:08:29.252
Then we're going to get into the advanced probability and statistics you can learn once you know calculus.

00:08:29.252 --> 00:08:32.973
Then we're going to learn linear algebra and matrix algebra, which are very useful for doing machine learning.

00:08:32.973 --> 00:08:37.518
And then of course, machine learning itself. We're going to cover all 3 big branches of machine learning.

00:08:37.518 --> 00:08:45.337
Supervised learning, unsupervised learning, and reinforcement learning.

00:08:45.337 --> 00:08:47.300
So if you're counting, that's like 19 certifications. Each of those takes about 300 hours. A conservative estimate.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:57.806
In practice, we had one person who went through 6 different certifications. They speedran it. It was an experienced developer, Florin Pop.

00:08:57.806 --> 00:09:06.162
He speedran the entire 6 certification in 29 days. That's about as fast you can probably do it. And this is 3 times that.

00:09:06.162 --> 00:09:18.789
So you're going to be hard-pressed to get through this in even a few years.

00:09:18.789 --> 00:09:24.748
Most likely you're going to be coming back to freeCodeCamp after you've been working, and you're going to continue to extend your skills.

00:09:24.748 --> 00:09:29.529
And that's what learning's all about. It should be life-long learning. So you're going to be able to put this

00:09:29.529 --> 00:09:35.780
life-long learning in action with a massive curriculum that will continue to help you grow as a developer.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:44.201
So let's take a look at what the curriculum actually entails. Just to give you an idea of the level of specificity and atomacity

00:09:44.201 --> 00:09:53.408
we've broken it down to. This is the web development curriculum. This is just a quick curriculum sheet. It shows you all the different topics

00:09:53.408 --> 00:10:03.815
we're covering just for the responsive web development certification. We make it very granular.

00:10:03.815 --> 00:10:11.973
We cover things in extreme detail because not only do we want to cover them but we want a lot of repetition

00:10:11.973 --> 00:10:17.056
so that you're applying and then later you're re-applying, and you're building lots of different projects

00:10:17.056 --> 00:10:27.731
to build your skills. And this sheet which I have link to by the way down in the description if you want to visit this yourself...

00:10:27.731 --> 00:10:35.671
If you go over to for example the relational database certification, which you're going to see a demo of in a minute

00:10:35.671 --> 00:10:49.363
We've got all the different bash commands that you're going to go through. A lot of them.

00:10:49.363 --> 00:10:49.863
SQL, Postgres, yeah. You're going to learn all these different commands, all these different concepts. You're going to keep applying them over and over.

00:10:49.863 --> 00:11:01.424
And another cool thing we're doing is we're taking these certification projects and instead of having all 5 of them at the end of the certification, we're going to have

00:11:01.424 --> 00:11:09.990
level, level, boss battle. Level, level, boss battle. We're going to try to mix up the gameplay a little bit.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:17.394
So you'll have hand-holdy tutorials where each step takes a minute or two to get through. Then you're going to have big

00:11:17.394 --> 00:11:26.101
ambiguous challenges where it might take you a few hours to figure out how to build this project.

00:11:26.101 --> 00:11:34.184
Both are very valuable.

00:11:34.184 --> 00:11:36.864
And again, we've got all these different certifications mapped out with varying degrees of development.

00:11:36.864 --> 00:11:45.576
For example, reinforcement learning. Markov chains, you're going to have a lot of fun with this.

00:11:45.576 --> 00:11:52.955
So this is in the link below. At this point want to hand it over to Eric who's going to demo what the freeCodeCamp learning experience is like for mathematics

00:11:52.955 --> 00:12:09.525
inside Jupyter notebook. Before I do that though, I just want to remind you. We are raising funds.

00:12:09.525 --> 00:12:15.823
We budgeted about $300,000 to bring on some instructors to focus on this intently and it's probably going to take about 2 years.

00:12:15.823 --> 00:12:23.323
So this is the budget for 2 years for bringing on a couple math instructors depending on how many of them are doing part time.

00:12:23.323 --> 00:12:33.223
A lot of instructors work at a lot of different schools.

00:12:33.223 --> 00:12:38.009
So we'd just like to build a really strong team of people who have expertise not just in these fields but in teaching these fields

00:12:38.009 --> 00:12:45.477
to help us build out these curricula.

